eneity  translates into features that are  relevant to response for a patient  to a given treatment regimen so a very  exciting time as  all of these technologies converge and  help us  both in the drug development process but  also  potentially in patient management  i'm going to move on now and talk about  adoptive cell therapy  and how biomarkers are relevant there so  adoptive cell therapy again  is a it's it's a increasing area of  interest  great success with products on the  market  uh addressing um  you know b cell uh malignancies uh  but again much like immunotherapies each  patient  uh may have a unique profile so we have  to look at biomarkers that are relevant  genotypic phenotypic that reflect both  the patient's cancer the nature of the  engineered therapy  the adoptive cells their car t molecules  as well as the response of that patient  and so um you know that because that's  challenging for us that we have to also  balance how these biomarkers are used to  effect  assess toxicity so cytokine release  syndrome  can be relevant for a very relevant for  patients with  receiving adoptive cell therapy  biomarkers of efficacy  and relapse  how all of these come together is a very  interesting story so  again it's critical for patient  identification  are they expression expressing the  target antigen of interest  for looking at pharmacokinetics and  pharmacodynamics  around the treatment dose the  persistence  and expansion of the cell therapy  when it's administered to the patient  does it engage the right  target is the target population  decreasing can we measure it is there  minimal residual disease  or have we eliminated the clone so  technologies that are relevant in  biomarkers that are relevant in this  space  are the assessment of the car t  molecules for example in our car t  project  or a car t therapy where we're looking  at both persistence  and expansion of the car t molecules  where are car t cells characterization  of the response to the care uh the car t  uh imminent phenotyping are we  activating  the appropriate immune response the pkpd  relationship  so the engineered cells being the  therapy  we're looking at those as well as the  tumor cells  that are the cartile therapy is targeted  against  to assess the level of the cartoon  molecule  as well as the response of the tumor  cells is it  are they decreasing are they increasing  etc  mechanism mechanistic markers of  efficacy  so if we measure the target androgen  uh cd19 positive uh b cells  are they decreasing as measured by flow  cytometry or by molecular diagnostics  patient id and stratification if we're  looking at  cd19 positive tumors or bcma positive  tumors  can we select and identify the right  patients and the middle  second row predictive biomarkers of  safety  response resistance or relapse so  minimal residual disease but  also looking at the immune response to  the adoptive cell therapy is it  a heightened immune response so you want  to activate the immune system  but you don't want to over activate it  so that you cause  challenges with that overall immune  response  uh expanding beyond the target  cancer cells of interest  so what's been suggested in  adoptive cell therapy is we take a  systems type approach  you have to measure multiple parameters  both of the therapy the engineered  cells of the tumor population  of the um response the immune response  of the patient  and those tools and technologies can be  very broad  they can be genomic technologies they  can be proteomics cell-based  technologies etc  and so again using those types of tools  and applying them to the challenges  uh and opportunities with adoptive cell  therapy  looking at again that interaction  of the engineered therapy  the target population and the patient's  immune response requires  again a different thinking than we've  applied in more  traditional oncology therapies  now for each of these whether it's a  biomarker assay one of those 300  or more that are in current drug labels  that look at safety  efficacy toxicity etc  whether it be a companion diagnostic  that's necessary for the safe and  efficacious use  of of a therapy we have a variety of  ways that we can commercialize  these assays many biomarker assays  uh our lab develop tests there are  assays that give us  information around the patient and the  patient's response to a therapy  but they are developed and offered by  laboratories with the appropriate  accreditation  there are examples where an in vitro  diagnostic company  a lab service provider in a  pharmaceutical or biotech company have  worked together in a three-way  partnership  linking a kit-based assay that can be  broadly distributed  post-approval of the drug and diagnostic  to link that development so that on day  one  you have the approval in a model  that allows access to testing and access  to therapy  on day one we also have  examples where again showing flexibility  of the regulatory process  of a single site uh offering the test  they take it through  for regulatory approval just like a  distributed kit  but it falls all within the quality  system  of that single location and we've got  examples of those as well  those often work very well for  novel technologies or  areas in which the overall patient  population  might be somewhat limited and then on  the right hand side of this slide  when you think of commercialization and  this is something  that pharma and biotech in vitro  diagnostic companies  labs have really started to collaborate  very effectively on  uh on day one when the  tests and therapy are available  there's a whole variety of things a a  very long  checklist that must be worked through in  order to  ensure success so is the lab up and  operational do they have the right  equipment do is the test on the test  request form  so that clinicians can order it are the  appropriate lab staff including  pathologists  in trained on the complexities or the  nuances of the essay  are reports ready to go out can we  take samples from  their collection in from the patient  liquid biopsy  tissue sample move them to the  laboratory appropriately  so that data can be generated and the  test results can go back  in a timely fashion so patients can  receive  the novel therapeutics that they are in  need of  can we educate and communicate  appropriately so that all parties  understand  how the test works and what value it  provides  are the client call points appropriate  so are we talking to pathologists are we  talking to oncologists  are we talking to healthcare providers  and payers  so that there's no weak link in the  chain  and then again is the right coding and  reimbursement strategy established so  it's a very  complex set of things that need to be  addressed and until very recently  it was often believed that you know day  one you put the test on your test menu  and everything is fine but there are  many many many things  that have to be um established  so that an effective use of the  therapeutic and diagnostic can  can happen  so my last slide so what i tried to do  today is talk a little bit about  how biomarkers we have to think about  them from  exploratory use mechanism of action  all the way through clinical development  into commercialization  we know that there's a variety of ways  of thinking  and technology is being applied  to how we look at biomarkers moving from  single gene  single protein to a more broad spectrum  multiplex technologies like ngs  like multiplexed immunistic chemistry  we know we have to consider the right  development and commercialization  strategy  will it be a lab developed test because  of its intended use  does it need to be a companion  diagnostic does  do we need to have a single location do  we need to be able to distribute that  product more broadly  we talked about incorporation into the  drug development process and how it's  iterative it's not  linear like it once was so biomarker  driven trial design and execution  application of biomarkers from  early on in the drug development process  through commercialization  the ability to expand these applications  to other areas outside of oncology  that being driven by better  understanding of disease biology  the relevant biomarkers examples rare  disease with gene replacement based  therapies  crispr technologies neurodegenerative  disorders  and other immunologically based  therapies  and lastly i would say that if we look  at the regulatory approaches  the flexibility to ensure  that we meet all of the standards that  are necessary  for both the diagnostic and the drug  development but  we look at how we can flexibly work  together  to advance these new therapeutics and  the associated diagnostics  in an appropriate and timely manner and  lastly  thinking of the global regulatory  environment that's an area that we still  need to work on as we think about the  development  of these tests in different geographies  whether it be the us  north america europe and asia pacific  so with that i will uh i'll conclude so  thank you for your time today it's been  a pleasure  being with you and presenting some of my  thoughts  yeah of where we are today and i think  where we're heading in the future  so thank you again
 

